EXTERNAL TOOLS — NOT BUNDLED
-----------------------------

  FFmpeg                    GPL-2.0+ / GPL-3.0 (typical builds)

    Voxframe does NOT bundle, link against, or statically incorporate FFmpeg.
    It invokes the binary already installed on the user's system as a separate
    process, exchanging data through files and pipes. This arm's-length
    invocation does not place Voxframe's source under the GPL.

    EXCEPTIONS: the Docker image, the Windows installer and the macOS app
    ship FFmpeg.

    - The Docker image is a combined work distributed under GPL terms. It
      documents this and carries the corresponding source offer.
    - The Windows installer puts FFmpeg (the gyan.dev "essentials" build,
      GPL-3.0) in its own folder beside Voxframe, as a separate program that
      Voxframe runs as a separate process. That folder holds FFmpeg's licence
      and SOURCE.txt, saying where the build and its source come from (D-158).
    - The macOS app does the same with a static FFmpeg build for Apple Silicon
      from ffmpeg.martin-riedl.de (GPL-3.0), in Contents/Resources/ffmpeg
      (D-161).

    SOURCE FOR THE BUNDLED FFMPEG (D-162). Instead of a written offer, every
    GitHub release carries the corresponding source beside the installers:

    - ffmpeg-<version>.tar.xz: FFmpeg's complete source for the version both
      installers ship, downloaded from ffmpeg.org by the release workflow and
      checked against a SHA-256 pinned in scripts/ffmpeg_sources.py (pinned
      after checking FFmpeg's release signature), with ffmpeg-<version>.tar.xz.asc.
      If the two installers ever ship different FFmpeg versions, both are
      attached.
    - ffmpeg-<version>-libraries.txt: for each build, the libraries compiled
      into it and their versions, as the build itself reports them (gyan.dev's
      README.txt; Martin Riedl's versions.txt), and where each build provider
      publishes their source: gyan.dev links each library to its project
      (https://www.gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/#libraries); Martin Riedl publishes
      the build scripts that download each library's source
      (https://git.martin-riedl.de/ffmpeg/build-script).

    Both builds are pinned by SHA-256 in the build scripts, so the source
    attached is the source of what ships. Each installer's SOURCE.txt, beside
    FFmpeg, points to that release and to the providers' source links.

    The PyPI package does not bundle FFmpeg and carries no GPL obligation.


